<h1>Shepherd Moons (1991)</h1>

<a class="new-amazon-album-link" href="https://amzn.to/3OCCMyY" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">
  <Image  src={sm} alt="Shepherd Moons (1991)"  width="300" height="300" /></a>

<h2>Album Overview and Commercial Success</h2>

<p>Enya's third studio album, <strong><em>Shepherd Moons</em></strong>, was officially released on <strong>4 November 1991</strong> in Europe by <strong>WEA</strong> and on <strong>19 November 1991</strong> in the United States by <strong>Reprise Records</strong>.</p>

<p>Serving as the direct follow-up to her 1988 breakthrough release <strong><em>Watermark</em></strong>, the album secured the Grammy Award for <strong>Best New Age Album</strong> in 1993.</p>

<p>The project achieved massive global popularity, selling over <strong>13 million copies worldwide</strong> and topping the <strong>UK Albums Chart</strong> at Number 1 while reaching Number 17 on the US <strong>Billboard 200</strong>.</p>

<p><a href="https://amzn.to/3OCCMyY" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Stream or download Enya songs</a></p>

<h3>Pressings and Track Variations</h3>

<p>The album exists in multiple distinct master pressings across different regions.</p>

<p>The initial 1991 pressings featured a 2:34 <strong>Gaelic language-only version</strong> of <a href="#Book_Of_Days">"Book of Days"</a>.</p>

<p>In 1992, Enya recorded a 2:55 bilingual <strong>English and Gaelic adaptation</strong> of the track for Ron Howard's motion picture <a href="/enya-songs-albums/#farandaway"><em>Far And Away</em></a>.</p>

<p>This dual-language recording permanently replaced the original Gaelic version on all pressings of <strong><em>Shepherd Moons</em></strong> manufactured from mid-1992 onward.</p>

<h3>Film Soundtracks and Key Singles</h3>

<p>Several compositions from <strong><em>Shepherd Moons</em></strong> were featured in major Hollywood motion pictures.</p>

<p>A remix of <a href="#Ebud%E6">"Ebudæ"</a> appeared in the comedy film <a href="/enya-songs-albums/#toys"><em>Toys</em></a>, while <a href="/shepherd-moons/#Marble_Halls">"Marble Halls"</a> was included in Martin Scorsese's 1993 drama <em>The Age of Innocence</em>.</p>

