<h2 id="linernotes">Liner notes</h2>

<h3>Shepherd Moons</h3>

<p>Two tiny new moons had been discovered orbiting an outer ring of <strong>Saturn</strong>.</p>

<p>Numbered <strong>1980S26</strong> and <strong>1980S27</strong> (later named <strong>Prometheus</strong> and <strong>Pandora</strong>), they worked ceaselessly on either side of the F ring to keep particles together like a shepherd guiding a flock.</p>

<p>The space probe <strong>Voyager</strong> sent their images to Earth, revealing a poignant and beautiful mystery operating silently in space.</p>

<p>Everyone had appreciated the spectacular beauty of Saturn, but where would the planet be without those precious, mis-shapen, miniature moons?</p>

<h3>Caribbean Blue</h3>

<p>It is much easier to imagine a beautiful place on a rainy day than to imagine a dreary one.</p>

<p>People may create something good merely by thinking it so, making a daydream as rich a gift as any.</p>

<p>Like <strong>Afer Ventus</strong> (the wind from Africa), <strong>Eurus</strong> (the East wind), <strong>Boreas</strong> (the North wind), or <strong>Zephyrus</strong> (the gentle West wind), the imagination is free to choose its own journey.</p>

<p>As with all dreams we reach for the ideal, and <strong>"Caribbean Blue"</strong> represents such a dream.</p>

<h3>How Can I Keep from Singing?</h3>

<p>This track is based on a 1868 Christian hymn composed by <strong>Robert Lowry</strong> and written by <strong>Pauline T</strong>, which Enya initially believed to be a traditional <strong>Shaker hymn</strong>.</p>

<p>The <strong>Shakers</strong> lived simple lives and wrote music with which they glorified their joy in living, reflecting beauty inherent in simplicity.</p>

<p>In 1846, the Shakers sent food to <strong>Ireland</strong> in an attempt to relieve suffering during the Great Famine, holding significance in the decision to arrange the piece.</p>

<p>The recording incorporates a third verse written by <strong>Doris Plenn</strong> and popularized by folk singer <strong>Pete Seeger</strong> during the 1950s.</p>

<h3>Ebudae</h3>

<p><strong>Ebudæ</strong> is the ancient Latin name for the <strong>Hebrides</strong> islands in western Scotland, referenced in Ariosto's classic tale <em>Orlando Furioso</em> as the Dreadful Isle or Isle of Tears.</p>

<p>The song's rhythmic arrangement is inspired by traditional Scottish <strong>waulking songs</strong> sung by women as they fulled cloth to the rhythm of their work.</p>

<p>Two distinct vocal layers thread through the track: one telling the narrative story, and another mixing half-invented, half-remembered phonetic sounds from childhood.</p>

<h3>Angeles</h3>

<p>At times we ask for extraordinary things to happen in our lives, believing deep down that they will occur.</p>

<p>Belief in a <strong>Guardian Angel</strong> or spiritual minder provides comfort and an easy relationship with a world beyond our own.</p>

<p>It offers a gentle hand to guide you through, though not away from, life's harder times.</p>

<h3>No Holly For Miss Quinn</h3>

<p>A partner piece to "Miss Clare Remembers" from <strong><em>Watermark</em></strong>, this instrumental was prompted by a story from author <strong>Miss Read</strong>.</p>

<p>The idyllic descriptions of village life recapture the innocence of an age far removed from modern society.</p>

<p>It suggests that listeners need to dip into that tranquil world from time to time.</p>

<h3>Book Of Days</h3>

<p>Inspired by Enya's personal habit of keeping daily diaries, the track examines the possibilities held within each blank page.</p>

<p>It reflects both sadness and exuberance when contemplating personal histories laid out in a journal.</p>

<p>The original Gaelic lyrics capture the anticipation and excitement of starting a new day.</p>

<h3>Evacuee</h3>

<p>The song was written after watching a <strong>BBC documentary</strong> about a girl evacuated from London during <strong>World War II</strong>.</p>

<p>Her emotional pain and tears while recalling her separation were as immediate half a century later as they were during her childhood.</p>

<p>The composition portrays a child's separation at a train station and the hope of being reunited with parents.</p>

<h3>Lothlórien</h3>

<p>This instrumental is named after the enchanted Elvish realm in <strong>J.R.R. Tolkien's</strong> <em>The Lord of the Rings</em>.</p>

<p>In Tolkien's legendarium, <strong>Lothlórien</strong> is a timeless land where golden autumn leaves of the Mallorn trees do not fall.</p>

<p>Historical translations of the name include "the vale of the land of singers", "the valley of the singing Gold", and "Dreamflower".</p>

<h3>Marble Halls</h3>

<p>Originally titled "I Dreamt I Dwelt in Marble Halls", this operatic aria was composed by Irish baritone <strong>Michael William Balfe</strong> for his 1843 opera <em>The Bohemian Girl</em>.</p>

<p>Balfe, praised by Johann Strauss as a master of melody, produced a piece that became a personal favorite of both Enya and her mother.</p>

<p>Producer <strong>Nicky Ryan</strong> used extensive acoustic reverb on the track so the listener feels as if they are standing inside a marble hall.</p>

<h3>Afer Ventus</h3>

<p>Translating from Latin as "African Wind", the song describes delicate moments when everything in life suddenly makes sense.</p>

<p>The sweeping melodic lines fit together to mirror James Joyce's literary concept of <strong>Epiphanies</strong>.</p>

<p>Although fleeting and brief, these moments remain crucial and revealing throughout a lifetime.</p>

<h3>Smaointe... (D'Aodh Agus Do Mháire Uí Dhúgain)</h3>

<p>The word <strong>smaointe</strong> translates from Irish literally as "thoughts".</p>

<p>The song reflects on a historical tsunami that destroyed a church and graveyard at <strong>Magheragallon Beach</strong> in Gweedore.</p>

<p>Enya's grandparents are buried in that graveyard, inspiring childhood memories and feelings of ancestral guidance.</p>

<p>Originally recorded in 1988 as a B-side to "Orinoco Flow", the track features <strong>Liam O'Flynn</strong> playing uilleann pipes.</p>

